\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A lifted reflection on a circle}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the [lift of the signed coordinate group](fdip-016P)
on a circle take #{S=\mathbb C^2}. Let the direction operator
exchange two chosen orthonormal basis vectors. Let #{\Gamma}
fix the first and negate the second, as in \ref{fdip-016L}.
Their product #{f_1} sends the first vector to the negative of
the second and sends the second to the first. Thus
#{f_1^2=-\bbI_S} and #{f_1^4=\bbI_S}. The group is cyclic of
order #{4}. Conjugation by #{f_1} negates the direction operator,
so its even powers project to the identity coordinate change.
The same calculation applies to a circle of period two, where
reversal fixes every site but reverses the labeled direction.}
